    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe region-and-hash redesign is wired through coherently \u2014 the audit line, check digit, and membership number all read `customerCode` and need no edits, and `formatLocality` reading the REGION segment from the code (with the postcode fallback) is a sensible, additive derivation. But the change introduces two structural problems in `ClinicServiceImpl`:\n\n1. **Duplicated SHA-256\u2192upper-hex helper** (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:305-316` vs `389-402`). The new `hash8` is a near-verbatim copy of the body of `householdIdFor`: same `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\").digest(...)`, the same `for` loop appending `String.format(\"%02X\", digest[i])`, and the identical `catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException) -> throw new IllegalStateException(\"SHA-256 is required but unavailable\", ex)`. The only difference is 4 bytes vs 8. This codebase already factors such concerns into small `util` classes (`LocalityResolver`, `AddressNormalizer`, `TelephoneNormalizer`, `LuhnCheckDigit`); the natural move is a single shared helper (e.g. a `util` method taking the number of bytes/chars) that both `generateCustomerCode` and `householdIdFor` call, rather than a second private copy living in the service.\n\n2. **Stale Javadoc on `countOwnersInCity`** (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:318-321`). It still reads \"used to derive the per-city sequence of the customer code,\" but this change removed the sequence entirely \u2014 `generateCustomerCode` no longer calls it, and its only remaining caller is `isCityAtCapacity`. Leaving the obsolete purpose in the doc misleads a maintainer about why the method exists; update it to describe the capacity-check role (the same way `toBusinessDay`'s doc describes its real consumers).\n\nMinor, same area: the `generateCustomerCode(String city, String lastName)` signature was replaced with `generateCustomerCode(Owner owner)`, which is a reasonable consolidation \u2014 no issue there.",
